# Pack the parameters, make the call to the underlying Java code, then return the result image

# Clear the return value to prevent accidentaly returning wrong data
Calculate ipReturnImage as #NULL

# Build a row which contains all of our parameters, it gets converted to JSON and sent to the worker
Calculate vrParams.picture as OXML.$base64encode(ppImage)
Calculate vrParams.scale as pnScale
Calculate vrParams.rotation as pnRotation

# Actually call our worker method
# - Module to use, which identifies the Java class we should call our function in
# - Method to call within that class (may have many methods for different funtions)
# - Parameters to pass in to our worker
# - Whether the worker call should run syncronously. I have this true, so I get a call to $methodreturn before execution continues here
# - Error text in case the worker call fails
# - Tag is unset here, since this example doesn't need it. Could be used to identify a batch job
Do $cinst.$callmethod(isModule,'TransformImage',vrParams,kTrue,vsErrorText)

# This value is set by $methodreturn. Since the worker call waited for completion before continuing, we know this value will be set
Quit method ipReturnImage